00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.800
California's 164-stall supercharger site draws power from an 11-megawatt solar array and 10-megapacks. This is Tesla Shorts Time, Episode 652. First principles, robotaxi hiring in Texas, and x-paying satisfaction ranking. Lost Hills, California now holds that supercharger on roughly 30 acres, according to Shattered. Tesla opened the first 84 stalls in July 2025, and finished the full build in late November 2025.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:36.840
Rows of covered stalls cross the lot. Solar canopies shade many parked cars, and a block of pull-through

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:42.880
bays serves drivers towing trailers. The station supports non-Tesla EVs that use the North American

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:50.020
charging standard, or NACS. Tesla owners Silicon Valley shot the tour, and Supercar Blondie called

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:55.800
the location arguably the largest while pointing to the Tesla diner in Los Angeles, as the more stylish

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.320
stop. The channel that filmed it also said the site is the first majority off-grid

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.736
supercharger. Once stall counts outrun what a local utility can reliably feed,

00:01:06.356 --> 00:01:10.256
on-site generation and storage set the limit on how many vehicles can charge

00:01:10.256 --> 00:01:14.636
at once. The charger stops being a simple tap on the grid and becomes a small

00:01:14.636 --> 00:01:19.396
power plant. Capacity can scale faster when solar and storage are built in from

00:01:19.396 --> 00:01:23.716
the start instead of waiting on a utility upgrade. The economic shift when

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.636
generation and storage are size to match the stall count.

00:01:27.136 --> 00:01:31.716
That capital has to be recovered through utilization fees rather than through vehicle sales.

00:01:32.296 --> 00:01:37.076
Each added stall raises both potential revenue and the buffer of stored energy needed for

00:01:37.076 --> 00:01:38.516
evening and overnight peaks.

00:01:39.056 --> 00:01:44.256
The design therefore rewards sites that hold high throughput across 20 hours, not sites

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:45.676
that only add more plugs.

00:01:46.176 --> 00:01:50.096
Layout that supports continuous running matters more than a spike at peak hour.

00:01:50.096 --> 00:01:55.176
The same constraint shows up anywhere fast-charging demand grows faster than grid upgrades.

00:01:55.836 --> 00:01:59.856
Operators who control both the vehicles and the charging hardware can match generation

00:01:59.856 --> 00:02:02.756
to fleet size without waiting for outside approvals.

00:02:03.436 --> 00:02:08.236
Operators who rely on third-party chargers take utilization limits set by someone else's

00:02:08.236 --> 00:02:09.136
investment schedule.

00:02:09.656 --> 00:02:14.156
At this scale, the difference shows up in daily energy throughput, not in marketing claims.

